<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toggenburg War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1712">1712</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Second War of Villmergen, Second Villmergen War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second War of Villmergen was a religious war of 1712 in the Old Swiss Confederacy, arising from a dispute in the Toggenburg between its Protestant subjects and the Prince-Abbot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-gallen">St. Gallen</a>. Zürich and Bern defeated the Catholic cantons at Villmergen on 25 July 1712, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-aarau-1712">Peace of Aarau</a> ended Catholic predominance in the common lordships.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toggenburg-war.jpg" alt="Toggenburg War" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sidonius · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_Zweiter_Villmergerkrieg_1712.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The County of Toggenburg had belonged to the Imperial Abbey of St Gall since 1460, and about two thirds of its people were Protestant after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. The abbots pressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> there, and in 1699 Schwyz proposed a road over the Ricken Pass, which would let Catholic troops reach the Toggenburg quickly. When Wattwil refused to build it by forced labour, Prince-Abbot Leodegar Bürgisser imprisoned the chief Toggenburg magistrate, Josef Germann, and in 1702 concluded a treaty of protection with Emperor Leopold I, which threatened to draw the abbey out of the Confederacy and into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> sphere.</p><p>In March 1707 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde">Landsgemeinde</a> at Wattwil adopted a constitution giving the Toggenburg self-government and religious freedom. Schwyz went over to the abbot, and by 1710 the Toggenburgers, armed with Zürich's help, held the abbey's fortresses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Bern and Zürich declared for the Toggenburgers in a manifesto of 13 April 1712, and the five inner cantons of Lucerne, Schwyz, Uri, Zug and Unterwalden armed in reply. Protestant forces took Wil on 22 May and occupied the Abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gall">Saint Gall</a>, whose abbot fled to Neuravensburg. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aargau">Aargau</a> the Bernese under Jean de Sacconay won near Mellingen and at Fischbach, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> surrendered on 1 June; its fortress, the Stein, was razed.</p><p>A first treaty signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aarau">Aarau</a> on 18 July was rejected by the Landsgemeinden of Schwyz, Zug and Unterwalden under the influence of the papal nuncio Giacomo Caracciolo, and fighting resumed. At Villmergen some 8,000 Bernese met 12,000 Central Swiss, and fresh troops and the superior Bernese artillery decided the long battle, in which 2,000 Catholics were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-aarau-1712">Peace of Aarau</a> of 11 August 1712, the Fourth Landfrieden, Zürich, Bern and Glarus kept the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and the lower Freie Ämter, linking Zürich with the Bernese Aargau, and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rapperswil">Rapperswil</a>, while Bern gained a share in every common lordship. The Second Landfrieden of 1531 lapsed, and Protestants received equal standing before the law. Zürich and Bern governed the abbey's lands and carried off much of its library. The Peace of Baden of 1718 restored the abbey under Abbot Joseph von Rudolphi, but some treasures, among them the globe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-gallen">St. Gallen</a>, stayed in Zürich until a settlement in 2006.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Toggenburg War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Catholic cantons won again in the First War of Villmergen in 1656, but Zürich and Bern were victorious in the <b>Second War of Villmergen</b> in 1712.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Military</a></li></ul></section>
